# dns: names to IPv4 addresses, served by the resolver (user/services/dns)
# on /svc/dns. A program whose list asks for it (`svc dns`) finds it
# there; init publishes it and keeps the shared channel's server end
# across the resolver's restarts. Each opener (libos's svc_open, svc_get)
# gets a channel of its own with the svc protocol's connect
# (abi/idl/svc.idl): an answer that comes later goes to that caller's
# channel only. The shared channel answers connect and nothing else.
# <dns.h> has the client side (dns_lookup) and the limits;
# docs/M9-PLAN.md "DHCP and DNS: processes of their own" the design.
#
# Each name in flight is a query of its own (its own socket, id and
# deadline), so a name whose server never answers delays only the
# callers asking for that name.
#
# Limits, so one program can't use the resolver up: DNS_OPENERS (16)
# openers (connect: ERR_NO_RESOURCES), DNS_PER_OPENER (8) resolves in
# flight an opener; 16 names in flight in all (the resolver's
# DNS_MAX_QUERIES, a socket each), 8 callers of one name. The fair
# shares: /svc/dns-sys is the same protocol on a second shared channel,
# for the network's own services (init grants it to sntp); the openers of
# /svc/dns together get at most DNS_PROG_OPENERS (12) openers, 12 names in
# flight and 6 callers of one name (ERR_NO_RESOURCES), the rest is the
# services' reserve.
#
# Reconnect: when the resolver ends (it does when netstack does), every
# opener's channel sees ERR_PEER_CLOSED; open /svc/dns again (svc_get
# does), whose connect waits on the shared channel, which init keeps, for
# the restarted resolver. Nothing is lost but its cache.
# Format: see tools/genidl.py.

protocol dns 30

# On an opener's channel: the IPv4 addresses of `name` (NUL-terminated in
# its 256 bytes; a host name, or a dotted IPv4 address, answered as it
# is), at most four, and how many seconds the answer may be kept (`ttl`).
# Answered when the answer comes, from the cache at once. Errors:
# ERR_NOT_FOUND (no such name, or it has no IPv4 address), ERR_TIMED_OUT
# (no server answered within the resolver's 10 s, or timeout_ms passed:
# 1..60000), ERR_IO (the servers failed), ERR_NOT_SUPPORTED (the answer
# needs TCP), ERR_OUT_OF_RANGE (more than 8 CNAMEs); at once:
# ERR_INVALID_ARGS (not a name, or no NUL; timeout_ms out of range),
# ERR_BAD_STATE (no DNS servers yet: see `net`), ERR_NO_RESOURCES (too
# many in flight).
1  resolve  (u8[256] name, u32 timeout_ms)  -> (u8 count, u32 addr0, u32 addr1, u32 addr2, u32 addr3, u32 ttl) later
